---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import PdfToJpg from '@/components/tools/pdf-convert/PdfToJpg.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="pdf-to-jpg">
  <PdfToJpg client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to convert a PDF to JPG</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Drop your PDF onto the box above, or click it to pick a file. pdf.js opens the document in this tab, and it is not uploaded.</li>
      <li>Choose a resolution. 150 DPI is the sensible default; pick 300 DPI if the images will be printed or run through OCR.</li>
      <li>Set the JPG quality if you need smaller files. Anything from 85 to 92 is visually indistinguishable from the maximum on a page render.</li>
      <li>Press <strong>Convert to JPG</strong>. Each page appears in the gallery as it finishes, so you can start checking results before the document is done.</li>
      <li>Download the whole set as a ZIP, or grab individual pages with the download button on each thumbnail.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Which DPI should you pick?</h2>
    <p>
      A PDF stores pages in points, where 72 points equal one inch. Rendering at
      a given DPI simply multiplies that grid: 300 DPI means each inch of the page
      becomes 300 pixels. The trade-off is file size, which grows with the square
      of the resolution.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>DPI</th>
            <th>A4 page (px)</th>
            <th>US Letter (px)</th>
            <th>Megapixels</th>
            <th>Best for</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>72</td>
            <td>595 × 842</td>
            <td>612 × 792</td>
            <td>0.5 MP</td>
            <td>Thumbnails, quick previews, email attachments</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>150</td>
            <td>1240 × 1754</td>
            <td>1275 × 1650</td>
            <td>2.2 MP</td>
            <td>Reading on screen, slide decks, web pages</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>300</td>
            <td>2480 × 3508</td>
            <td>2550 × 3300</td>
            <td>8.7 MP</td>
            <td>Printing, OCR input, archival copies</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Higher is not automatically better. Rendering cannot invent detail: if the
      page contains a 150 DPI scan, requesting 300 DPI just doubles the pixels
      and adds no information. Vector pages (made by Word, LaTeX, InDesign or a
      browser's print dialog) do get sharper, because the
      text and lines are re-drawn at the higher resolution.
    </p>

    <h2>When JPG is the right choice</h2>
    <p>
      JPG uses lossy compression tuned for photographs. It is excellent for pages
      that are mostly images and poor for pages that are mostly text at low
      quality settings, where you will see faint "ringing" halos around letters.
      For text-heavy pages that must stay pristine, use
      <a href="/pdf-to-png">PDF to PNG</a>. It is lossless, but the file is much
      larger.
    </p>
    <p>Typical reasons people rasterise a PDF page:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>To post a page online where a PDF viewer would be clumsy: a menu, a flyer, a certificate.</li>
      <li>To put a page into a slide deck or document that cannot embed PDFs cleanly.</li>
      <li>To flatten a form, so the fields, annotations and layers become a fixed picture.</li>
      <li>To feed OCR software, which usually wants 300 DPI images, not a PDF.</li>
      <li>To send a preview of one page without the whole document.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What stays on your device</h2>
    <p>
      Everything. pdf.js parses the PDF in a web worker, each page is drawn on an
      HTML canvas, and the browser encodes the canvas to JPG. No part of the file is
      sent, and there is no queue. If you disconnect from the network after this
      page has loaded, the tool still works, which shows that nothing is uploaded.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
